## Why This Is Asked
This question tests memory ownership and pointer traversal across language boundaries in a practical CGO task.

## Key Concepts
- CGO memory management (malloc, strdup, free)
- Converting C linked lists to Go slices
- Proper free semantics and ownership transfer
- Concurrency safety with CGO calls using mutex synchronization

## Code Example
```c
/* C header and implementation */
typedef struct Node { const char* value; struct Node* next; } Node;

Node* build_list(const char** items, size_t n) {
    Node* head = NULL;
    Node* tail = NULL;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        Node* node = malloc(sizeof(Node));
        node->value = strdup(items[i]);
        node->next = NULL;
        if (tail) tail->next = node;
        else head = node;
        tail = node;
    }
    return head;
}

void free_list(Node* head) {
    while (head) {
        Node* next = head->next;
        free((void*)head->value);
        free(head);
        head = next;
    }
}
```

```go
// Go binding
package main

/*
#include "header.h"
*/
import "C"
import (
    "sync"
)

var mu sync.Mutex

func BuildList(items []string) ([]string, error) {
    mu.Lock()
    defer mu.Unlock()

    cItems := make([]*C.char, len(items))
    for i, s := range items {
        cItems[i] = C.CString(s)
    }
    defer func() {
        for _, c := range cItems {
            C.free(unsafe.Pointer(c))
        }
    }()

    head := C.build_list(**C.char(&cItems[0]), C.size_t(len(items)))
    if head == nil {
        return nil, nil
    }

    var result []string
    for node := head; node != nil; node = node.next {
        result = append(result, C.GoString(node.value))
    }
    C.free_list(head)
    return result, nil
}

func main() {
    var wg sync.WaitGroup
    wg.Add(2)

    go func() {
        defer wg.Done()
        result, _ := BuildList([]string{"a", "b", "c"})
        println("Goroutine 1:", result)
    }()

    go func() {
        defer wg.Done()
        result, _ := BuildList([]string{"x", "y", "z"})
        println("Goroutine 2:", result)
    }()

    wg.Wait()
}
```

The mutex ensures serialized access to C functions, preventing race conditions when multiple goroutines call BuildList concurrently.

